","The REVISIONISTS, plaintiffs and appellants below, moved this revision under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the decree dated 21.10.2020 of the learned appellate court, which affirmed the decree dated 26.01.2015 of the learned trial court in their suit of 2013 against the STATE and other OPPOSITE PARTIES. They sought a permanent injunction restraining dispossession from certain pond lands, case that a superior landlord settled them by patta on 15.01.1352 BS to their predecessor in 1946, that he obtained a decree in 1981, was permitted to mutate the ponds, and paid rent up to 1419 BS. The STATE denied the claim, contending that the ponds were the landlord's excess property vesting in it by operation of section 20 of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950. The REVISIONISTS relied on Exhibits 7 and 7(1), rent receipts and six witnesses.",Other: State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950,REFUSED,"The court found no misreading or non-consideration of documents by the courts below. The REVISIONISTS had never challenged the S. A. record of rights published after 1950 in the Government's name as Khas Khatian, and their earlier decree and mutation could not establish title once the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950 came into force, lands held by a rent-receiver beyond the permitted area being annexed to the Government. Its six witnesses, including the first REVISIONIST, public witnesses and an official producing registered volumes, did not prove entitlement, while the STATE produced one witness. Concurrent findings on the evidence disclosed no error of law, so the court declined to interfere.",bc921b8109dc56602ad4fdd868ddab8b80f56ded836df8f4ac16f20d1424ab14
