","The PETITIONERS, importers and purchasers of one hundred and forty-seven four-stroke CNG three-wheelers, filed this writ petition under Article 102 of the Constitution against the STATE, the Road Transport Authority and the Chairman of the Regional Transport Committee, Sylhet, challenging the resolution of that Committee's meeting of 16.05.2019 not to register their vehicles. They had applied for registration during 2014 and deposited the fees for new registration, tax token and fitness, and they sought declarations that the resolution was without lawful authority with directions to issue registration certificates, transfer of ownership, tax tokens and fitness certificates. They relied on their own earlier Rule, on the apex court's disposal of the matter, on a legal opinion of the Authority dated 30.08.2016 permitting registration, and on accrued loan interest and threatened auction of their mortgaged property. The STATE answers that they never obtained the Authority's permission.","Constitution of Bangladesh, Article 102",ALLOWED,"The court held the resolution arbitrary and without lawful authority: the Committee may deal with route permits only and had no power to decline registration. Its order reflected neither the apex court's direction, which concerned vehicles unaccompanied by route permits, nor the Authority's legal opinion of 30.08.2016 allowing the Sylhet office to register the vehicles absent any restraining order, and the earlier assurance to the applicants was ignored. Refusing the lawful owners would not stop unregistered vehicles already plying the roads, while the exchequer lost tax from genuine purchasers. The court declared the order of no legal effect, directed issue of the documents, and required the unauthorized vehicles to be brought within the regulatory framework.",acedfe4530be7a938a55868436b6786281fb6e46d74fe36c9a2773f24b9b1433
