","The PETITIONER, plaintiff in a title suit of 2022, moved this revision under section 115(4) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the order dated 19.10.2023 of the learned District Judge in Civil Revision No. 44 of 2023, which disturbed an order dated 03.08.2023 by which the trial court had stayed operation of an ex parte decree passed in an earlier suit, Title Suit No. 33 of 2014, the legality and propriety of which decree his pending suit challenges. His stay application, issued in his favour below, having been disturbed in revision, he sought restoration of that protection. The OPPOSITE PARTIES, respondents in the suit, support the impugned order. Both sides commended a restraint order for a limited period to enable early settlement, and counsel for the OPPOSITE PARTIES raised a question of jurisdiction under the Civil Courts (Amendment) Act 2021.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"Invoking the apex court's view that a stay should issue for a limited period to avoid multiplicity of proceedings, the court directed the parties to maintain status quo as to transfer of the suit property until disposal, and required the trial court to decide the suit within six months. No view was taken on the merits of the disturbed stay order. Separately, it read sections 2 and 19 of the Civil Courts (Amendment) Act 2021 as fixing pecuniary competence at Tk. 25,00,000 for the Senior Assistant Judge and Tk. 15,00,000 for the Assistant Judge, found the suit valued at Tk. 12,34,000, and directed that matter be placed before the District Judge for transfer under section 19(2).",3162baf82ee3e7ae54e27f33235145d0e1f43404d6b1857c1ef5f630aab231e0
