","The REVISIONISTS, heirs of a pre-emptee, moved this revision under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the order dated 13.06.2012 of the learned appellate court, affirming the order dated 19.08.2001 of the learned trial court in a pre-emption matter. The application, founded on section 96 of the State Acquisition and Tenancy Act 1950, was made against a registered deed No. 11643 dated 30.11.1991 by which heirs of a C. S. recorded co-sharer conveyed an eight-anna share for a nominal sum. The REVISIONISTS say the instrument was a Nadabinama taken to clear a cloud from misrecording in the survey settlement, not an out-and-out sale, relying on a settlement in 1351 B. S., sixty years of possession, a separate R. S. khatian, a 1989 mutation and rent receipts. The pre-emptor pleads purchases in 1963 and 1967, knowledge in 1992, and the trial court's findings.",Transfer of Property Act 1882,ALLOWED,"The court held that the courts below misread the record in treating the instrument as a sale. The other side's witnesses admitted the REVISIONISTS' predecessor held the land long before the deed; the scribe and an attesting witness deposed that it was drawn to remove confusion from wrong recording and that no price passed. A deed requiring attestation under section 3 of the Transfer of Property Act 1882 must, by section 68 of the Evidence Act 1872, be proved through an attesting witness, and neither executant contradicted the REVISIONISTS, who discharged the burden under sections 101, 103 and 106, peculiar knowledge resting on the person holding it. Oral proof was admissible to show whether a transfer was truly a sale.",0cc65abc5f8c212e089b410707eedc5901fb2f1e54e63734545f013381ee146f
