","The REVISIONISTS, heirs of the defendants, moved this revision under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the decree dated 26.09.1999 of the learned appellate court, which reversed the decree dated 30.04.1997 of the trial court in Title Suit No. 56 of 1992, brought by OPPOSITE PARTY No. 1 for declaration of title and recovery of khas possession over sixty-six decimals. The plaintiff traced title to a kabala of 16.03.1992 from heirs of a tenant under the superior holder, pleaded dispossession in April 1992, and denied that a decree in Title Suit No. 693 of 1974 bound him. The defendants relied on purchase from an heir of the superior holder, on that decree and its affirmance in appeal, and on res judicata, limitation and vesting as non-resident property. The REVISIONISTS say the appellate decree does not engage with the trial court's findings.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court read the plaint as showing that the plaintiff's father and uncle were parties to earlier suits decided against them, that the defendants were thereafter mutated and paid revenue, and that the survey record placing the land in the Government's name was never questioned. A plaintiff must stand on his own title, not on weaknesses in the defence, and where a suit is barred by law it may be disposed of under Order 7 rule 11 or section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908. The plaintiff's witnesses threw no light on the date of dispossession, and the appellate court, reversing without addressing the trial court's reasoning, produced no proper judgment of reversal.",6a62f9846351fdaa11e015df9cf194144527651bf5581d422b5973ed6178f70a
