","The REVISIONISTS, plaintiffs in a suit for declaration of title and recovery of possession, moved under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the order dated 06.02.2025 of the learned District Judge, Dhaka, affirming the order dated 10.03.2024 rejecting their application under Order 39 rules 1 and 2 for temporary injunction restraining the OPPOSITE PARTY, defendant. They say that during pendency the defendant is raising a multistoreyed building, selling flats to be constructed and mortgaging the property to a bank, and that the trial court overlooked that part of their land stands wrongly recorded in his name in City Jorip Khatian No. 229. Invoking prima facie case, balance of convenience and irreparable loss, and urging sufficient cause for their 288-day delay in appeal, they seek direction to maintain status quo and try the suit expeditiously. The defendant answers that one out of possession cannot seek injunction.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court applied the familiar requirements for interim relief, prima facie case, balance of convenience and irreparable loss. The plaintiffs' deed of 2009 covered plots within City Jorip Khatian No. 183, while S.A. Khatian No. 44, R.S. Khatian No. 40 and City Jorip Khatian No. 229 stood for another plot in the defendant's name, and whether any portion of the one had migrated into the other remained open for evidence and local investigation. No prima facie case on title or possession thus existed. The appellate court was correct in finding the appeal, delayed by 288 days without sufficient cause, inadmissible, and any transfer during the suit being hit by section 52 Transfer of Property Act 1882 no injunction was needed.",5289e0e225f7c232db4b312fed0d7d4e50b7aa07ec846f7295ca65d665e10f93
