","The REVISIONISTS, heirs of the contesting defendants, moved under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the decree dated 15.09.2021 of the learned District Judge, Kurigram, in Other Appeal No. 50 of 2018, reversing the decree of 2018 in Other Suit No. 137 and decreeing the OPPOSITE PARTY's suit for title to 15 decimal land. The suit recites purchase of that parcel in 1986 from one of two brothers owning 79 decimal under S. A. Khatian No. 268, possession through a caretaker, and dispossession on 30.08.2015; the REVISIONISTS claim the whole through a deed of 1959 to their predecessor, his mutation, holding No. 3581 and standing huts. Nobody appeared for them, while the opposite party relied on his proved deed, mutation and rent receipts, on the missing registry pages clouding the 1959 deed, and on the caretaker never examined.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court recorded that the 79 decimal belonged to two brothers in equal shares, that the REVISIONISTS' predecessor mutated his name long before the plaintiff's purchase of 1986, and that neither transferor nor their heirs questioned the 1959 deed covering the larger area. It held that a plaintiff suing for declaration with recovery of possession must establish lawful title together with prior possession and dispossession by legal evidence. The plaintiff's deed of 1986 stood proved, but his own account placed custody with his caretaker, who went unexamined without explanation, leaving possession and dispossession unestablished. The appellate court's contrary appreciation ignored that legal aspect and could not stand, so the trial decree was restored.",c54ff6a5ce9b2dfb1d4696e3080b40777f00bdb9f9058f4864fbab7089b13e26
