","The REVISIONIST, plaintiff below, moved under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the decree of 20.05.2013 of the learned Additional District Judge, Netrokona, in Other Class Appeal No. 64 of 2011, reversing a decree whereby his suit for rectification of a registered kabala deed of 04.06.1998 had been decreed. He alleged that the parties agreed to convey 8.5 decimals of Plot No. 159, belonging to the father of the OPPOSITE PARTY, who had transferred his whole holding in Plots Nos. 160 and 161, covering 45 decimals, but that through the deed writer's error or the vendor's collusion the instrument described those last plots. Six registered deeds and three witnesses stood on one side, one witness on the other. In appeal it was said that the vendor conveyed what he owned, that the suit came ten years late, and that neither error nor fraud was made out.",Other: Specific Relief Act 1877,REFUSED,"The deed on its face conveyed Plots Nos. 160 and 161, and the plaintiff bore the burden of establishing error or fraud in its description. The vendor's father, admitted owner of half the 45 decimals, had conveyed only 10 decimals, leaving a subsisting interest in 12.5 decimals descended to the OPPOSITE PARTY, so the premise of the claim, a wholly exhausted holding, failed; neither plaint nor witnesses named quantity or transferee. The scribe and persons present at the bargain went unexamined, one witness owned ignorance of the position at execution, and possession, taken north of the boundary wall where the plots lay, agreed with the instrument. The appellate decree disclosed no illegality.",b18cb8d431a0eb8592e28e3214e182d887dbf865ab43317a7702a8e930c79f51
