","The PLAINTIFFS-REVISIONISTS, in a partition suit, moved under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the order of 08.02.2024 in Title Suit No. 695 of 2023, allowing a defendant's application to recall the compromise decree of 09.11.2023, and obtained a Rule with a stay. They say the Code empowers no trial court to recall a decree on compromise, the application named no provision, civil revision was the defendant's proper course, recalling the decree while retaining the compromise petition is self-contradictory, and the order book records no appearance by him. Other opposite parties say the suit is partition with a preliminary decree only, leaving him free to claim his share. He answers that his appearance was kept out of the record, the application should construe as one under section 151, and appeal from a compromise decree is barred under section 96(3).",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found nothing in the order book or later orders recording an appearance or request for time by the defendant, while on that very day the suit was decreed on compromise after examining witnesses. The trial court, recalling the decree yet retaining the compromise petition, acted in a slipshod, self-contradictory way lacking legal basis, the application citing no provision. Appeal lay barred under section 96(3) of the Code, leaving other recourse open, and doctrine settled in 60 DLR (AD) 38 forbids resort to the inherent power under section 151 where an alternative remedy exists; the jurisdiction was exercised on the court's own volition, beyond its provisions. The Rule succeeded, without costs.",e18a69eaf2acd585ea5e5c18d4e85de0ee43c8d945a416a23dc583b84d1894b4
