","The PETITIONER, defendant No. 6 in a title suit, moved under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the order of 22.01.2019 of the learned Joint District Judge, Dhaka, rejecting her application under Order VII rule 11(a) and (d) with section 151 for rejection of the plaint, obtaining a Rule and stay. The plaintiffs, OPPOSITE PARTIES Nos. 1-3, sue for title to 19 katha on an agreement for sale of 1974 and a power of attorney. She urges that the same parcel was decreed in her favour in a suit of 1992, that decree surviving appeal, leave and review; that the agreement was there disbelieved; that the schedules cover the same quantity; that these plaintiffs stood defendants there; and that this suit aims at harassment, a plaint being rejectable under section 151 where ineffective on admitted documents. The earlier appeals were pursued by impersonators.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court examined the clauses of Order VII rule 11, noting the application named no law barring the suit nor cause of action want, yet found the record decisive: title stood declared for the PETITIONER below in the earlier suit, two of the three reliefs sought were disposed of in her favour there, the schedules covered the same quantity, and the plaintiffs had been defendants, so no suit rests on a document found void. Following 53 DLR (AD) 12 and 21 BLC (AD) 218, it held rejection open under inherent power where, on admitted facts, the suit is barred, no court standing helpless to prevent abuse of process. The order bore no legal substance.",bd869c61539f5e81765ad8198e0cef69a9d89a2cf651d33ec97250b92dd783a8
