","Two revisions by heirs of contesting defendants, under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure, sought to displace the decree of 05.04.2010 of the learned Special District Judge reversing the decree of 1984 in a suit for title to a pond in Plot No. 280 of an akhra estate. The RESPONDENTS' title rests on a rent receipt from the secretary of the deity and three deeds, one unregistered for Tk. 95. The REVISIONISTS say the suit is time-barred, the deeds forged or antedated or covering other plots, the deed disclosed first with the plaint, the schedule vague, the land non-agricultural so that section 23 of the Non-agricultural Tenancy Act demanded registration. The RESPONDENTS answer that the receipt is thirty years old under section 90 of the Evidence Act, that a deed under one hundred takas needed no registration before 2004, and that a pond yields joint possession.",Registration Act 1908,ALLOWED,"The court read the rent receipt as genuine, corrections in other ink proving no fabrication, while the defendants' own receipt, ball-pen though dated 1362 B.S., stood discredited; no appeal having followed the trial decree against them, their title ended there. It sustained the unregistered deed, section 17 of the Registration Act then compelling registration above one hundred takas only, yet noted its endorsement naming a stranger and unlike signatures on the registered deeds, so expert comparison was essential; omission to seek a handwriting opinion was the lapse complained of. Limitation ran from the abatement of the 1966 suit. A pond admits no division, so ejmali sufficed. The appellate decree gave way, the appeal reopening on the single question.",09951084498c5d51fd0453c0278f3857fa1ab17ae249e2bc5186de63d79b31ff
