","The REVISIONIST, defendant in a suit for injunction, moved under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure against the order of 30.01.2024 of the learned Additional District Judge, Chattogram, affirming the order of 13.04.2023 restraining him from dispossessing the OPPOSITE PARTY, changing the character of 24 sataks, cutting trees or disturbing her possession, and directing him to remove structures and vacate within twenty-one days. He says the land is undivided ejmali property inherited by himself and his sister, who conveyed her 8 decimal share to the plaintiff by a deed of 2001 with a covenant for shortfall, that the plaintiff took no possession, cut no trees and shows no prima facie case, and that no injunction lies against a co-sharer lacking partition. He relies on two commissioner reports, the plot vacant with trees before the status quo order, sixteen pillars and a roof afterwards.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found that the defendant, in objecting to the mandatory injunction, never denied disobeying the status quo order, entering the land, cutting trees or raising the structure, his sole answer being that an undivided co-sharer cannot be enjoined, a question for decision on evidence; and that the commissioner found the plot vacant and afterwards bearing sixteen pillars with a cast roof. The trial court held the act deliberate and unpardonable, regard being had to the authority of the courts, and the appellate court concurred. Continued construction would render the suit itself redundant, and under the principle in 16 BLC (AD) 26 a party foreclosing the court's order with a fait accompli may be compelled to restore the prior condition.",91ca843f810941be91d92cc2c597fcd53d6b914559296e075d451b1437e7a332
