","The REVISIONISTS, heirs of a deceased defendant, moved under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908 against the order dated 09.05.2013 of the learned Joint District Judge, 1st Court, Naogaon, in Miscellaneous Appeal No. 53 of 2012, affirming the order dated 30.04.2012 declining relief under Order 9 rule 13 against an ex-parte decree of 1987 in a partition suit of 1976, and obtained a Rule. They say summons never reached defendant Nos. 22 and 30, that their case came to their knowledge only when disclosed, that the burden of proving service lay on the plaintiffs, who produced no return because the record had been destroyed, and that both courts below misread the materials; their witness, they add, could speak no further. The respondents say the appellate court, from the order book and register entries, rightly held service proved.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court recorded that the defendants admittedly never appeared or contested at any stage, that the witness called for the respondents spoke of appearance and abandonment, an account contradicted by the record, and that he claimed no service of summons; no attempt was made to prove service by oral or documentary evidence. The appellate court, it held, went beyond pleadings and evidence in treating an entry as proving service on every defendant except two bands of numbers, a finding unsupported and untenable. The decree having been passed without service, the two orders below were displaced, the ex-parte decree fell, the suit stood restored to its file, and the trial court was directed to conclude the hearing within six months.",f4ceb840d3ac843267cc9c28a9e4d0cc78373b60993c29cb6ed364b8094a4edc
