","The PLAINTIFFS-REVISIONISTS, claiming as heirs of PERSON_1, sued for declaration of title and confirmation of possession over 1.67 acres held by him under a registered patta of 1945. The suit was decreed, that decree gave way in Title Appeal No. 61 of 2009 and they moved under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908, obtaining a Rule against the judgment and decree dated 04.07.2010 of the learned Joint District Judge, 3rd Court, Patuakhali. They say PERSON_1 died issueless, his gift to a boy he raised, unregistered, could not be read in evidence, the purchases tracing through it failed, and the revenue record was erroneous; heirship was pleaded defectively and possession unproved. The OPPOSITE PARTIES answer that the plaintiffs proved no title, the record resting on two registered deeds over thirty years old. Mohammedan law on gift and the Registration Act 1908 were urged.",Other: Mohammedan law (gift),REFUSED,"The court found the genealogy neither pleaded nor proved: the plaint omitted the fathers of the nephews said to inherit, no date of PERSON_1's death was stated, PW1, a child of eight at that demise, knew neither PERSON_2 nor PERSON_3, and the patta recorded different fathers for the other allottees, so cousinhood failed; the plaintiffs' mode of possession went unmentioned. The defendants produced rent receipts and registered kabla deeds of 1949 and 1950 underlying the khatian, their evidence consistent. An unregistered instrument requiring registration is inadmissible, but a Muslim gift before 2004 needed neither writing nor registration, declaration with delivery completing it; the appellate appreciation called for no interference and the Rule was discharged without costs.",ea4e3bbab394119a753fa3f0c72ea8f46359b8fe2ae0fda7df16ca70fa9a9a11
