","The PLAINTIFFS-REVISIONISTS sought declaration and a permanent injunction over 1.5 acres, their predecessor having taken a Government settlement under a registered kobuliyat of 17.12.1980, cancelled in 1987 on private claimants' petition; the suit was decreed, that decree gave way in Title Appeal Nos. 81 and 92 of 2019, and they moved under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure 1908, obtaining Rules heard together against the decree dated 09.12.2021 of the learned District Judge, Patuakhali. They say the land stood recorded as Government property in S. A. Khatian No. 1 together with possession by huts and cultivation, the cancellation made without hearing or jurisdiction, and the appellate court reversed no material findings. The RESPONDENTS answer that most of the plots was private, bought at auction sale in 1980, that the Government held no subsisting title, and that a decree of 1969 on prior title suits settled the recording.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The kobuliyat covered land recorded in the Government's name, and the cross-examined Assistant Union Land Officer showed only small private parcels in the two plots, the rest khas, so the private-title premise failed. The prior title suit, decreed in 1969, named no plot quantities and omitted the khatian from its schedule, settling nothing over that land. Possession by huts and cultivation went uncross-examined and was corroborated, a defence witness, though declared hostile, speaking of it uncontradicted; the authorities could not call the published survey record erroneous; the enquiry report behind the cancellation was never admitted or tested. The appellate decree was indefensible; the court made both Rules absolute without costs and restored the trial decree.",910682d882de5394ebe1d8ac286f129f1e368917c5b180790b0a68f84dafc701
