","THE IMPORTER-PETITIONER brought hot rolled steel sheets from China under two letters of credit and cleared them on final assessments of 15.06.2022, then obtained a rule nisi under Article 102(2)(a)(i) and (ii) of the Constitution calling on the revenue respondents to show cause why the provisional assessment of 03.08.2022 reopening those files, and the two bank guarantees of Tk. 19,82,069 and Tk. 16,79,292.99 exacted for release of the goods, should not be declared without lawful authority with the guarantees returned; the rule carried a restraint on encashment. The Board answers that prime quality goods were mis-declared as secondary, that the importer itself sought release on guarantee, that the writ is premature pending the test report and adjustment at final assessment, and that delay and unclean hands bar relief.",Other: Customs Act 1969,ALLOWED,"The Customs Act, 1969 was read as a closed scheme: section 80(1) governs assessment on examination or test, section 80(2) allows assessment on the bill's statements with re-assessment if they prove incorrect, section 81 permits provisional assessment only where duty cannot immediately be assessed, against security, with final assessment within 120 working days, and section 83A allows amendment even after the goods pass out of control. Nowhere does the law permit provisional assessment after final assessment. Recourse lay to sections 80(2), 83A or 32, not to a demand beyond the statute, and estoppel cannot enlarge statutory power (62 DLR (AD) 298). Quality and quantum were for the customs authorities. The rule succeeded, the guarantees to go back, the respondents at liberty to proceed by law.",ca50462a9601e4c982e89e971d0e421b5b5f31c7518f9b30206af6e9363ad216
