","THE MILLER-PETITIONER, having repaid by 26.08.2013 the whole of the Tk. 4,70,00,000 drawn in 2012 from a sharia-based bank's Jatrabari branch against a composite investment limit, complained in 2020 to the central bank that no clearance certificate had issued; the central bank's special Audit and Inspection Team reported on 05.05.2021 with fifteen recommendations, the last directing the respondent bank to refund Tk. 1,22,58,750, and a writ filed on 30.01.2023 had brought a direction to dispose of the petitioner's representation within thirty days. It now sought under Article 102 of the Constitution a declaration that the Assistant Director's letter of 15.03.2023 staying that report was without lawful authority, with a direction to refund; the banks answer forged export bills for Tk. 13,42,95,200 claimed elsewhere, three money suits pending, suppression and unclean hands.","Constitution of Bangladesh, Article 102",PARTLY,"The court held that the report of 05.05.2021 came from a competent team after notice to both sides, which the respondents did not deny, and that fraud vitiates everything was no substitute for a hearing: review by a higher authority demands a law so providing, none was shown, and the petitioner admittedly went unheard even on the forgery charge, so the stay letter offended natural justice and Article 31 and fell as without lawful authority and of no legal effect. The report did not thereby spring into force, being a rebuttable document for the civil court; the sum stood unadmitted, and claim, counter-claim and the pending suits raised disputed facts unfit for summary writ jurisdiction. The rule was disposed of with observations only.",e312492687e6fac500d77b01b477605cfed5016ced674a6fc836d66510fe47e6
