","THE COATED-STEEL IMPORTER-PETITIONER brought goods described as painted, varnished or coated with plastic from China and, after the final assessment of 10.04.2022 on its bill of entry fixed taxes at Tk. 36,36,520.90, obtained a rule nisi under Article 102(2)(a)(i) and (ii) of the Constitution against the provisional assessment compelling bank guarantee No. SEBL/IMG/BG/2022/03 of 04.07.2022 for Tk. 24,18,904.10, with the respondents restrained from encashing it. It says the respondents' own office file, a copy whereof it collected, shows the provisional notice issued on 09.06.2022, weeks after the final assessment stood complete, and that the guarantee was taken under duress to free the consignment for production; the revenue answers mis-declaration of quality, the importer's own application for release on a continuing guarantee, prematurity pending the test report, and unclean hands.",Other: Customs Act 1969,ALLOWED,"The Customs Act, 1969 supplied the only pathways: re-assessment under section 80(2) where the bill's statements prove incorrect, amendment under section 83A even after the goods pass out of control, and proceedings under section 32 for an untrue statement. Nothing in the Act, the bench found, permits a provisional assessment once the final assessment stands complete, nor could good faith or the importer's request supply the omission. Estoppel was no answer, an authority's power being bounded by the four corners of the statute and not enlargeable by conduct (16 BLD (AD) 67; 62 DLR (AD) 298); the goods' quality and the duty in consequence lay with the customs authority. The rule succeeded, the guarantee to be returned, the respondents at liberty to proceed by law.",a62d5c184106335f73c885554ac24070ec71fa0bceae35b511000dbe85c3f394
