","THE STEEL-SHEET IMPORTER-PETITIONER brought hot rolled steel sheets from China under three letters of credit of March and April 2022, filed four bills of entry between 23.05.2022 and 19.06.2022, and had them finally assessed on 24.05.2022, twice on 25.05.2022 and on 19.06.2022 at Tk. 28,25,179.70, Tk. 21,66,434.27, Tk. 15,12,500.25 and Tk. 6,36,526.09 in taxes, in that order. It then obtained a rule nisi under Article 102(2)(a)(i) and (ii) of the Constitution against the four provisional assessments compelling four bank guarantees of Tk. 29,59,111.79, Tk. 15,83,122.69, Tk. 22,68,595.53 and Tk. 6,65,089.59, with a restraint on encashment. The revenue answers that prime quality was concealed behind a secondary declaration, that the importer itself sought release on continuing guarantees, and that the petitions are premature.",Other: Customs Act 1969,ALLOWED,"Four final assessments stood complete; no head of power in the Act raised four guarantees. The bench traced section 80(1), the re-assessment in section 80(2), the provisional assessment in section 81 (open only where duty cannot immediately be assessed, final assessment to follow within 120 working days), the amendment power in section 83A and the offence in section 32, and found no warrant for reopening closed files to exact security. The importer's own applications could not create one, estoppel not stretching statutory power (16 BLD (AD) 67; 62 DLR (AD) 298). The sheets' quality and the duty in consequence were for the customs authority. The rule carried the day, all four guarantees to go back, the respondents free to proceed by law.",6839948a53cbe1fbc9b8063de37665a2290789d42709eebf9b478f4364a56092
