","THE IRON-MERCHANT PETITIONER brought hot rolled steel sheet from China under letters of credit of 20.03.2022 and 24.03.2022, filed bills of entry C-688231 and C-865507 of 13.04.2022 and 17.05.2022, and closed final assessments on 13.04.2022 and 18.05.2022 at Tk. 30,34,140.45 and Tk. 32,00,453.93 in taxes. Holding the goods back thereafter, the revenue respondents issued the impugned provisional assessments and took bank guarantees Nos. 47/2022 and 48/2022 of 29.05.2022 and 20.06.2022 for Tk. 31,20,310.41 and Tk. 33,52,485.23, whereupon the importer obtained a rule nisi under Article 102(2)(a)(i) and (ii) of the Constitution and a restraint on encashment. It says the guarantees were extorted while port charges mounted; the Board answers that the consignment was mis-declared, that the importer asked for the course adopted, and that no cause of action arose.",Other: Customs Act 1969,ALLOWED,"The two consignments were assessed and the files closed on 13.04.2022 and 18.05.2022; the demands followed the petitioner's own written requests of 23.05.2022 and 15.06.2022 for a chemical test and for release against continuing security. Reading sections 80, 81, 83A and 32 of the Customs Act, 1969 together, the bench found no authority to substitute a provisional assessment for a completed final one, however convenient to the importer, and none buildable on consent, public bodies acting within the four corners of their power. Good faith on the officer's part was beside the point, the sample having been retained for examination, the differential liability still to be fixed. Effect went to the rule, both guarantees to be returned, the respondents free to re-assess or proceed.",16fc101fc8158d61a673f420fc878f2f04a555f5e6f792a2ed6cd707324d95f7
2250-2270,WRIT PETITION NO. 9911 OF 2022,2024-08-01,"
