","THE PETITIONER MANUFACTURER imported hot rolled steel sheet in coil, stated in secondary quality under a heading of HS Code 7208.39.20, from China under a letter of credit of 28.03.2022 against a proforma invoice of 25.03.2022 at USD 560 per metric tonne, filed bill of entry C-905454 on 22.05.2022, and was told on 30.05.2022 that taxes of Tk. 2,04,02,694.58 fell due. Before any adjudication order issued, the customs respondents imposed a provisional assessment and, on the importer's application of 20.06.2022, took bank guarantee No. 123BG01221720001 of 21.06.2022 for Tk. 94,51,501.39, releasing the goods against it. This Court was moved under Article 102(2)(a)(i) and (ii) of the Constitution to strike that course down and return the guarantee, the rule carrying a bar on encashment.",Other: Customs Act 1969,ALLOWED,"Two features marked this petition: the assessment came from a differently numbered customs respondent, and the guarantee from a bank arrayed last, the goods going free only on that security. The bench held the reopening of a completed assessment unlawful: sections 80(2), 83A and 32 of the Customs Act, 1969 mark the routes open where a bill's statements prove incorrect, and nothing followed from the importer's application, the officer's good faith or estoppel, an authority not enlarging its power by conduct (62 DLR (AD) 298). The quality of the coils and the duty consequent upon them remained for the customs authority under the Act. The rule was given effect, the guarantee to be returned, the respondents at liberty to proceed in accordance with law.",e78fcf5ff2861d7b46a6e568ff9ee8f292410fc452ef2a9bba8187d26575cdd4
